WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.620
- Welcome back, we have here
Kevin James and Renata Notni.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.780
Our next guest not only
played a talented singer

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.610
and songwriter on the hit show Nashville,

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.470
he is one in real life as well.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.560
His debut solo album is
called Love Ain't Pretty.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.840
It's available now wherever
you get your music.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.190
So check it out, everybody say hello

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.457
to friend of the show Charles Esten.

00:00:19.457 --> 00:00:20.681
(audience cheers)

00:00:20.681 --> 00:00:23.681
(upbeat rock music)

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.120
All right, so they gave me extra,

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.183
'cause we love Charles.

00:00:46.183 --> 00:00:47.016
- Thanks for the extra.

00:00:47.016 --> 00:00:48.030
- [Kelly] Oh yeah. (laughs)

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:49.830
I didn't know this about
you, but you start,

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:51.420
and I feel like I've known you for a bit,

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.010
but like you started on Broadway.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:54.390
I didn't--
- I started on Broadway.

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:55.280
- I didn't finish it. (laughs)

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.570
- I know, but I didn't know that.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.106
- Well, yeah I mean, I love that what you,

00:00:59.106 --> 00:01:00.434
New York suits you.

00:01:00.434 --> 00:01:02.371
- I love New York.
- Well I can tell that, Kelly.

00:01:02.371 --> 00:01:03.204
- Yeah.

00:01:03.204 --> 00:01:04.650
- It comes right through the screen and it

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:05.790
suited me for a quick minute.

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:07.260
I was in Los Angeles and auditioned

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.600
for the show called Smokey Joe's Cafe.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.913
It was a revue of the songs
of Leiber and Stoller.

00:01:11.913 --> 00:01:13.530
So great, really--
- I performed that.

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:14.430
- In pop choir in high school.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:15.263
- You did?
- Yeah.

00:01:15.263 --> 00:01:17.790
- Well, years before that I
auditioned for the Broadway show

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:20.400
and I just had a really great audition.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.680
For years before that, I
would get a vocal audition

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:23.970
for a Broadway show and they'd go,

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.530
all right, they wanna see a callback.

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:27.120
And I go, for what?

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.180
And they say, oh, they,
it's a dance audition.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.840
I go, well, I don't dance,

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.940
so I probably shouldn't go do that.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.340
- Yeah, just show up
and lie and say you can.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:36.360
- No, that's what they said.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:37.710
They just wanna see if you can move.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.240
And apparently I can't move.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:42.450
But anyway, this one audition,
I just was free and loose.

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:43.530
I did a couple Elvis songs.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:44.580
Hound Dog, Leiber and Stoller

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.023
wrote Hound Dog and Jailhouse Rock.

00:01:46.023 --> 00:01:46.856
- Yeah.

00:01:46.856 --> 00:01:48.300
- And so I think I bamboozled them.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.130
And then I went here on Broadway

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.440
and we were rehearsing
in the studios downtown.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.540
And I looked around and I
was surrounded by people

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:57.060
that were just legit Broadway talent.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:00.120
My leading lady was DeLee
Lively and she was unbelievable.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.740
Such a great dancer, singer.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.990
And then I think it was
about three weeks in,

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.270
the director finally showed
up to see where we were.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:09.603
And I could just see on
his face, him watching me.

00:02:10.507 --> 00:02:12.564
You know that thing where you
have like imposter syndrome

00:02:12.564 --> 00:02:14.730
and you're like, I don't belong here.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.740
And everybody's gonna figure that out.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.955
- Still have it.
- I have that often.

00:02:18.955 --> 00:02:20.815
- Well, they figured it out. (laughs)

00:02:20.815 --> 00:02:23.160
He looked at me and he
said in front of everybody,

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:23.993
he stopped the whole thing.

00:02:23.993 --> 00:02:27.780
He goes, "Let me ask you
a question: Do you dance?"

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.947
And I said, "Well Jerry,
that falls under things

00:02:29.947 --> 00:02:31.568
"you should have asked at the audition."

00:02:31.568 --> 00:02:33.464
(all laugh)

00:02:33.464 --> 00:02:35.520
And, and I said, I'm doing my best.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:38.070
I will get it by showtime,
I promise you that.

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:40.740
About a week later, he gave
me a very nice phone call

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.360
and he said, we really
appreciate your hard work,

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.260
but we're gonna have to--

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:44.093
- Aw.

00:02:44.093 --> 00:02:45.510
- [Charles] So they recast the role.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:46.533
- Oh.

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:49.050
- And the first thing I found
out was that I had a contract.

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:50.460
So I got paid for a year,

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.860
for not being in that show.
- Well that's great.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.210
- That's like the one time
- Amazing.

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:55.530
- you're thankful for contracts.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:56.627
- It was unbelievable.
- That's amazing.

00:02:56.627 --> 00:02:58.950
I'm at six months in, I go to my mailbox,

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:00.150
I'm like, honey, I got a raise.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:01.647
I gotta bump at six months.

00:03:01.647 --> 00:03:03.180
So, and another thing I found out is,

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.700
if I had done that show, I
would not have been available

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.677
to get on Whose Line is it Anyway?

00:03:07.677 --> 00:03:09.060
- Oh I love that.
- Which changed my life,

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.100
- being on that show
for a number of years.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.560
And then a final couple, two things.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.420
Years later, six or eight years later,

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.730
we're walking some fair in LA,

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:21.360
and we see DeLee Lively and her husband,

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.544
who was the guy they replaced me with.

00:03:23.544 --> 00:03:24.510
- Oh.
- Aw.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:26.010
- All these things had to happen.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:27.060
- They had three kids.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:29.040
They had one in her arm,
one in the stroller,

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.660
one walking, and they were amazing.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:31.493
And we talked and it was really,

00:03:31.493 --> 00:03:33.180
and I go, I think it's very important

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:34.740
that you guys understand and know

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.660
and remember that you have all this bliss.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.090
You have this paradise
because Charles Esten

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:40.051
can't dance.
- Can't dance.

00:03:40.051 --> 00:03:41.478
(all laugh)

00:03:41.478 --> 00:03:42.311
(audience applauds)

00:03:42.311 --> 00:03:43.728
- You're welcome.

00:03:44.863 --> 00:03:47.250
So this is your first solo album.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:49.620
So are you excited about
that or nervous about that?

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:50.670
- I'm really excited.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:51.900
You know, by the time it's out,

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:53.490
you got, you just want to get it out.

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:55.530
I put out a ton of singles over the years.

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:56.363
- That's all me, yeah.

00:03:56.363 --> 00:03:57.196
- When, you know, when
I went to Nashville,

00:03:57.196 --> 00:04:00.000
it really opened music
all the way open again.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:00.960
Like it used, it was a door

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.240
that I sort of stepped away from it.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:03.720
It was what I did first in college.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.160
I was in a band and all this thing.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:06.810
But I ended up doing the
acting and that drew me away.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.210
I would improvise songs on
Whose Line or things like that.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:11.910
But, so from then I started
putting out a bunch of singles.

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:14.460
I think I've always held the
album as something special,

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:16.530
'cause I grew up with albums that--

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:17.460
- Actual stories.
- You know.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:18.480
- Like the whole, yeah.
- They meant something.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.100
- Like when when you'd listen on the radio

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.680
and you'd hear a song and
you immediately knew the song

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:23.848
that followed it on the album.

00:04:23.848 --> 00:04:24.681
- That's right.
- Yeah.

00:04:24.681 --> 00:04:26.310
- You could hear it coming
or you knew a little bit.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:28.920
And so albums like Born to Run,

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:30.000
where it was like a movie to me,

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.560
and here comes Meeting Across the River,

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.420
and Jungle Land and all these things.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:36.120
So I sort of held the
album in such high regard

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:37.740
that I wanted to take some time.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:40.620
I wanted to figure out
who I am as an artist.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:41.880
You knew that instantly.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:43.228
I'm so you just, your voice.

00:04:43.228 --> 00:04:44.127
- You know what, I didn't.

00:04:44.127 --> 00:04:44.960
- You didn't?

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:45.793
- No, 'cause you know why?

00:04:45.793 --> 00:04:46.626
I love everything.

00:04:46.626 --> 00:04:47.760
I love every kind of music,

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:48.677
so that's the hard thing about me.

00:04:48.677 --> 00:04:50.970
- So you could sing like
the other great singers

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:52.868
and you had to slowly not sing like them?

00:04:52.868 --> 00:04:54.060
- Well I just mean like
finding your own sound.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:55.320
Like when someone says,
what is your sound?

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:56.430
I'm like, I don't know.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:57.390
Like I don't, you know, I guess pop.

00:04:57.390 --> 00:04:58.620
What's popular.
- That's crazy to me.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:00.180
- Because I would, I
would think you would,

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:01.980
it seems to me you know,
but I'm the same way.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:04.050
Like I've sung like so many other people.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:06.387
I started off playing Buddy
Holly in the West End.

00:05:06.387 --> 00:05:08.970
And on Whose Line you'd be singing a song

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:11.760
like Sting, or Bono, or
Neil Young or whatever.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:13.980
So by the time I got to Nashville
I was trying to figure out

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:15.298
who I am, what I wanted to say.

00:05:15.298 --> 00:05:16.624
- I think that's always evolving as well.

00:05:16.624 --> 00:05:17.820
- Yeah. That's it.
- 'Cause it's your--

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.220
- Who you're inspired by as well, yeah.

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:22.020
Do you remember like your first, like,

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.480
I don't know, your first
concert you ever went to,

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:25.740
your first time you were inspired

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:27.660
by an actor that made you wanna act?

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.120
- Yeah, I mean, I watched,
you know, standup specials.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:32.430
Robert Klein was a comedian that you know,

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:35.010
and when you start doing
standup, you kind of start

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:37.890
doing, taking on their
cadence and stuff like that.

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:38.940
Until you kind of

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:39.773
find yourself.
- Yeah, we're all imitators

00:05:39.773 --> 00:05:40.785
- Exactly.
- Until you find yourself.

00:05:40.785 --> 00:05:41.618
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:41.618 --> 00:05:42.623
- So that was, yeah, for sure.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:45.120
- Okay, everybody, Charles is performing

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:46.140
for us later so stick around.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:47.550
It's so good.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:48.720
All the lines are good in it.

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:50.010
It's like very well written.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:53.220
And up next it's a story that's
very personal for Charles.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:54.930
We're gonna meet an incredibly brave kid

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:56.910
who fought cancer and won.

00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:57.743
Yes. (applauds)

00:05:57.743 --> 00:05:58.576
(audience applauds)

00:05:58.576 --> 00:06:00.120
Now she's helping other kids do the same.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:01.380
We'll be right back.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:03.420
- Years ago Charles went through an ordeal

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:05.207
that no parent wants to experience.

00:06:05.207 --> 00:06:06.540
At two and a half years old,

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:08.730
his daughter was diagnosed with leukemia.

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:10.050
Thankfully she got through it,

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:11.880
and today she is cancer free. (applauds)

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:13.640
(audience applauds)

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:16.290
But Charles knows how
difficult that rollercoaster

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:18.300
is for any kid and for their family.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:20.310
That's why he wanted
to meet our next guest.

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:22.894
She too is a childhood
cancer survivor. (applauds)

00:06:22.894 --> 00:06:23.727
(audience applauds)

00:06:23.727 --> 00:06:25.319
She's also the founder of a huge nonprofit

00:06:25.319 --> 00:06:27.540
that helps kids and their families

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:29.970
worldwide cope as they battle cancer.

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:34.169
At age 15 she is one of our
youngest Rad Humans ever.

00:06:34.169 --> 00:06:38.704
(upbeat rock music)
(audience applauds)

00:06:38.704 --> 00:06:41.760
Alright, y'all from the Koenig
Childhood Cancer Foundation,

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:43.564
please welcome Elana.

00:06:43.564 --> 00:06:45.372
(audience applauds)

00:06:45.372 --> 00:06:46.580
Meet Charles.

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:47.503
- Hello, Elana.

00:06:47.503 --> 00:06:49.244
- Oh my God.
- It's nice to meet you.

00:06:49.244 --> 00:06:50.202
- It's so nice to meet you.

00:06:50.202 --> 00:06:51.132
- Nice to meet you too.

00:06:51.132 --> 00:06:51.965
- I'm such a big fan.

00:06:51.965 --> 00:06:52.950
- Oh, that's so sweet.

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:53.783
Thank you so much.

00:06:53.783 --> 00:06:55.673
She's a fan of Jesse
on the Disney Channel.

00:06:55.673 --> 00:06:57.020
- Oh I love it!
- I played the dad on Jesse.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:58.200
- Yeah. Yeah. Yeah.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:00.000
- That's amazing. I forget that part.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:00.833
I forget that.
- Yeah, I know.

00:07:00.833 --> 00:07:02.144
- It was such a long time ago.

00:07:02.144 --> 00:07:02.977
- It's, yeah, it was.

00:07:02.977 --> 00:07:04.050
You were just a little kid then, right?

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:04.883
- I know.

00:07:05.797 --> 00:07:07.860
- You're so old and wise now.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.410
So how old were you
when you were diagnosed?

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:12.600
- Yeah, so I was actually diagnosed right

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.993
before my eighth birthday in 2016.

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:19.440
It was such a stressful time.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:21.870
My mom was sort of the
first one who realized

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:25.380
that something was wrong with
me because I was limping.

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:27.330
She brought me to the
hospital immediately.

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:29.250
She rushed me to get help.

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:30.810
I obviously, I didn't wanna go,

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:35.010
because hospitals are
scary for kids my age.

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:37.140
But when I got the diagnosis,

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:39.543
everything sort of changed after that.

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:43.170
I had to live in the hospital
for one and a half years.

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:46.530
I had nine cycles of chemo, 15 surgeries.

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:49.590
It was the most significant
time of my life.

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:52.890
I can't even express
how much it affected me.

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:56.744
But luckily I'm six years
cancer free right now.

00:07:56.744 --> 00:08:00.363
(audience applauds)

00:08:00.363 --> 00:08:02.940
- I can't, it's hard on you
and it's hard on your family.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:05.400
I imagine that your family,
like what y'all went through

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:06.990
and your daughter was diagnosed like that.

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:07.823
- Absolutely. Yeah.

00:08:07.823 --> 00:08:09.660
- You, you were in and
out right, of hospitals?

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:12.300
- Well, when I hear her
tell the story, it's,

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:15.450
our daughter was two and a
half when Addie was diagnosed

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:18.210
and she had about two years
of chemotherapy herself.

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:19.170
There she is.

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:20.490
And so, but I've always thought

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:22.860
it was an incredible blessing
that she was so young

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:24.810
that she didn't really
understand any of it.

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:26.910
So it was like a new
day and she didn't know

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:28.710
she was gonna be losing her hair.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:30.870
She didn't know that she had
a spinal tap the next day.

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:32.550
And that was a real
blessing when I see kids

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:34.294
who are a little bit older,
- They're aware.

00:08:34.294 --> 00:08:36.240
- that Elana was old enough to understand

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:38.070
so much of what was going on.

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:40.080
So it always blows my mind.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:41.640
But I always find again and again,

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:44.103
when I meet these kids that
have been through this,

00:08:44.103 --> 00:08:46.170
that they, you can already
tell she's different.

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:48.270
She has this strength,
she has this charisma,

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:49.470
this power and intelligence.

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:51.510
So I'm not surprised at all.

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:52.343
- Yeah.

00:08:52.343 --> 00:08:54.330
Explain the foundation to everybody.

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:56.100
- Yeah, so I'd like to think

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:58.500
that I turned my pain into an opportunity.

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:01.920
I started it in 2020 with
the support of my family.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:03.772
I couldn't have done it without them.

00:09:03.772 --> 00:09:07.890
I wanted to help the kids in the hospital

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:10.200
who I had connected with
during my treatment.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:15.200
It started with that with
only $900 in my piggy bank.

00:09:15.270 --> 00:09:16.103
But then it sort of--

00:09:16.103 --> 00:09:17.411
- That's a pretty good piggy bank.

00:09:17.411 --> 00:09:18.390
(all laugh)

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:19.770
I don't know my piggy bank ever made that.

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:22.080
I bought too much music, yeah.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:23.580
- I know, after that it grew

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:26.466
into a $1.5 million charity.

00:09:26.466 --> 00:09:27.960
- Geez.
- Yeah.

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:31.380
We help kids financially, emotionally.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:34.500
We have gift bags that we
deliver to the hospital,

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:37.800
that we put items in based
off of what I went through.

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:42.360
We have socks, squishies, handmade cards,

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:45.450
brochures if they wanna register for help.

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:47.280
We pay for medical bills.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:51.510
We've helped over 30,000
families worldwide.

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:54.000
We've delivered 3,500 goodie bags.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:57.040
We've had 2,500

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:01.080
celebrations and visits
with kids in the hospital.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:04.590
I'd like to think that
all we do is for the kids.

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:08.880
Especially knowing how far
I've grown since I beat cancer.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:10.470
- Yeah, you beat it.

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:11.645
I love saying it like that too.

00:10:11.645 --> 00:10:12.690
Yeah. Yeah.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:14.580
So you, you, I don't know,

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:17.490
you got your message out in
a very creative way, I feel.

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:19.359
Like you were singing the
national anthem, right?

00:10:19.359 --> 00:10:21.180
How did that idea come about?

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:23.820
- Yeah, so I have been connected

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:26.610
with music long before I was diagnosed.

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:31.230
I've been taking singing classes
since I was six years old.

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:32.063
- Yeah.

00:10:32.063 --> 00:10:34.020
- I sang to other kids in the hospital.

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:35.730
I sang in talent shows.

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:37.590
I'd like to think it,
you know, soothed them.

00:10:37.590 --> 00:10:40.260
It helped them as well as it helped me.

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:43.560
So after I started my
foundation I you know,

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:45.210
got opportunities like the Mets game

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:46.890
which was very exciting.

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:49.890
That was a vital moment
in sharing my experience,

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:53.580
sharing my message, the cause.

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:55.740
And since then I've been using my voice

00:10:55.740 --> 00:10:57.750
to help kids in the hospital.

00:10:57.750 --> 00:10:59.640
- Yeah, well I know that Charles,

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:01.200
you wanna say something to Elana, right?

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:02.033
- Well, absolutely.

00:11:02.033 --> 00:11:05.370
First of all, I think
sometimes you meet people

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:06.750
and you go, this is somebody

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:09.480
where cancer messed with the wrong person.

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:10.680
Because you're coming back

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:13.443
and fighting it on
behalf of so many others.

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:16.230
Because of our daughter
Addie, my wife Patty

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:18.810
and I started years ago,
a thing called Team Addie.

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:20.400
And we raised money for the Leukemia

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:23.460
and Lymphoma Society with Team Addie.

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:24.570
And my question to you is,

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:25.890
have you been to Nashville before?

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:26.970
Have you been to Music City?

00:11:26.970 --> 00:11:28.230
- I have not.

00:11:28.230 --> 00:11:30.810
- Well, I was wondering if we
could fly you to Music City

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:33.210
and you could be a part of
our next benefit concert.

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:34.096
Would you do that?

00:11:34.096 --> 00:11:35.769
(audience applauds)

00:11:35.769 --> 00:11:36.638
Would you do that?

00:11:36.638 --> 00:11:37.471
Oh, hey.

00:11:41.381 --> 00:11:42.422
Please say yes.

00:11:42.422 --> 00:11:43.499
Aw, sweetheart.

00:11:43.499 --> 00:11:45.438
- I love it.
- Thank you, love.

00:11:45.438 --> 00:11:47.563
- We appreciate that.
We thank you so much.

00:11:47.563 --> 00:11:48.721
- You're gonna love it.

00:11:48.721 --> 00:11:50.037
If you like music, and I think people

00:11:50.037 --> 00:11:52.950
always think when Nashville
they just think country music,

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:56.820
which it is obviously very
much a core part of that city.

00:11:56.820 --> 00:11:58.620
But everybody's there.

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:00.960
Like rock and roll people,
singer/songwriters,

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:01.793
blue singers.
- Absolutely.

00:12:01.793 --> 00:12:02.820
- Like, it doesn't, pop singers.

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:03.870
I lived there for like 12 years.

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:05.880
Like every, it's just a place you can go

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:07.650
if you love music and it's therapeutic.

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:09.690
There's so many shows to see,

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:12.510
so many places and people
to meet, to network with.

00:12:12.510 --> 00:12:14.156
It's a really cool, you're
gonna, you're gonna love it.

00:12:14.156 --> 00:12:14.989
- Yeah. I'm so excited.

00:12:14.989 --> 00:12:16.860
- Yeah. Thank you Charles. (applauds)

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:17.910
- Oh, thank you guys.

